  :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-09.edn"
  :doc/body "# ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 09 - the read was split, and the half ayatori would fix is already O(result)

> Iteration 08 measured materialize O(database) against cursor O(result) and
> said in its own limits section that \\\"the cursor fixes the read\\\" was still a
> projection, because a kotobase read is snapshot + novelty and the production
> 25s had never been split. Seed 1 was to split it. This is that split, and it
> reverses the conclusion 08 was pointing at.

## R. Same total quads, only the fold point moves, `limit 1`

`bench/snapshot_vs_novelty.cljk`, against kotobase-peer's own `hot-datoms`:

    arm                            total  unfolded |  gets  decrypts  rows
    all folded (snapshot only)       400         0 |     4         1     1
    half unfolded                    400       200 |   217       201     2
    all unfolded (novelty only)      400       400 |   426       400     1
    all folded, 4x bigger           1600         0 |     4         1     1
    all unfolded, 4x bigger         1600      1600 |  1701      1600     1

**The snapshot half is already O(result).** Four block reads and ONE decrypt
for a `limit 1`, and it does not move when the snapshot quadruples: 400 quads
and 1600 quads both cost 4 and 1. `cold-datoms` already prefix-scans the
prolly tree and honours the limit.

**The novelty half is O(unfolded), exactly.** decrypts == unfolded count at
every point measured. `hot-datoms` enumerates every not-yet-folded tx block,
decrypts all of them, builds a db from all of their quads, and only then
applies `opts` -- so `:limit` and `:components` bound the ANSWER and not the
WORK.

## What this reverses

Iteration 08's numbers are correct and its comparison was against
`arrangement.core/restore`. **kotobase's read path does not call `restore`.**
It calls `cold-datoms`, which already does what a cursor does. So the
O(database) arm 08 measured is not the arm production runs, and the cursor
would optimise a half that already costs 4 reads and 1 decrypt.

That does not make 08 wrong about ayatori. It makes 08 wrong about which
problem ayatori was the answer to, which is the thing 08 flagged as unmeasured
and this iteration was supposed to settle. It settled it the other way.

**The lever is the novelty half, and it is the half with no cursor at all.**
Everything the snapshot side has -- prefix scan, limit, early stop -- the
novelty side lacks, and iteration 08's seed 3 already named that gap without
knowing it was the whole cost.

## Why production looks exactly like this

`yoro-social-v2` (root ADR-2609100100): 75 blocks, reads that decrypt 200-400
rows and burn 25s, a fold that decrypts ~1725, `gets:0` on a 24s read because
every block was already cached, and fewer than 100 trampoline handler runs.
Every one of those is the signature of the bottom row of the table above --
work proportional to unfolded novelty, with I/O already absorbed by caches.

It also explains the deadlock this graph is in without needing anything else:
`do-transact` runs a policy scan through the same `hot-datoms`, so a write
pays the same full novelty read; the write therefore cannot finish; auto-fold
is only scheduled after a SUCCESSFUL write; and folding is the only thing that
shrinks novelty.

## Seeds for iteration 10

  - **Bound the novelty read by the query.** The tx blocks are content-
    addressed and each carries its own quads; a `limit` can stop enumerating,
    and `:components` already has an exact subject index (the 2026-09-03
    prune) that today narrows which blocks are DECRYPTED but only after
    `novelty-cids` has walked all of them. The prune and the limit both exist;
    neither reaches the enumeration.
  - **Ask whether a novelty cursor is possible at all.** The snapshot is a
    prolly tree and admits a prefix cursor. Novelty is a cons chain of tx
    blocks in commit order, which admits a bounded PREFIX walk but not a
    key-ordered seek. \\\"Stop early\\\" and \\\"seek to a key\\\" are different asks and
    only the first is obviously available.
  - The fold threshold is still unswept (07's seed). It is now clearly the
    other lever: it sets how large the O(unfolded) term is allowed to get."}]
